From: "Ycarus (Yannick Chabanois)" <ycarus@zugaina.org>
Subject: [PATCH] mptcp: pm: in-kernel: batches of 16 subflows on x86_64

Since the in-kernel PM limits were raised to 64 subflows and 255
endpoints, subflows created in reply to an ADD_ADDR, or for the fullmesh
endpoints, still come in a single batch of at most MPTCP_PM_ADDR_MAX (8),
the size of the arrays on the stack.

OpenMPTCProuter on x86_64 aggregates up to 16 WANs: use batches of 16
there. That makes the frames of mptcp_pm_nl_add_addr_received() and
mptcp_pm_create_subflow_or_signal_addr() 632 and 520 bytes, which is fine
with the 16KiB x86_64 stacks. The other targets keep 8.
---
 net/mptcp/pm_kernel.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

--- a/net/mptcp/pm_kernel.c
+++ b/net/mptcp/pm_kernel.c
@@ -29,7 +29,15 @@ struct pm_nl_pernet {
 	DECLARE_BITMAP(id_bitmap, MPTCP_PM_MAX_ADDR_ID + 1);
 };
 
+/* Size of the batches in which subflows are created in reply to an ADD_ADDR
+ * or for the fullmesh endpoints. x86_64 routers can aggregate 16 WANs, and
+ * have the stack for it: create all their subflows in one batch.
+ */
+#ifdef CONFIG_X86_64
+#define MPTCP_PM_ADDR_MAX	16
+#else
 #define MPTCP_PM_ADDR_MAX	8
+#endif
 #define MPTCP_PM_SUBFLOWS_MAX	64
 
 static struct pm_nl_pernet *pm_nl_get_pernet(const struct net *net)
